</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Settings</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the proposed PTS against three categories of baselines:
(1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Different data patterns.</span> Using the same image source, we construct two alternative instruction datasets: one that only includes the final numerical answer (<em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Direct</em>), and another that contains conventional chain-of-thought annotations synthesized by GPT-4o (<em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">CoT</em>). These datasets are used to train the base LVLMs with both SFT and GRPO.
(2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spatial-aware LVLMs.</span> Models explicitly designed to estimate spatial information such as object sizes in real-world scenarios, including Spatial-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang, 2025</a>)</cite> and SpaceThinker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">remyxai, 2025</a>)</cite>.
(3) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool-augmented LVLMs.</span> Approaches that integrate external tools (<em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> visual experts or Python code) to provide auxiliary visual information, such as Visual Sketchpad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and DetToolChain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Implementation details are presented in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Implementation Details ‣ Unleashing Perception-Time Scaling to Multimodal Reasoning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.
For backbone models, we select the state-of-the-art LVLMs Qwen2.5-VL-3B and Qwen2.5-VL-7B, and implement our method on them.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluated Benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate model’s performance on DisTANCE and other out-of-domain benchmarks: (1) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DisTANCE<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{ood}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">ood</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{ood}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>: we replace the images in DisTANCE shapes not included in the collected training data (<em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> trapezoids and pentagons); (2) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geoperception</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: a fine-grained perception benchmark. We select the LHC subset which requires the model to compare the length of two segments in geometric images; (3) <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LEGO-Puzzles</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>: a spatial understanding benchmark with LEGO-based tasks, where we select the Height subset that asks the model to distinguish the relative heights of 3D LEGO objects.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Experimental results on out-of-domain distance reasoning tasks. Geo<math id="S4.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{LHC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>LHC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{LHC}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the LineComparison subset from Geoperception. LEGO<math id="S4.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{Height}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>Height</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{Height}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Height subset from LEGO-Puzzles.</figcaption>
