</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Analysis on Reward Factor <math id="A3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We illustrate the curves of the linear reward and the exponential reward under different values of <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#A2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix B Definition of Perception Ratio ‣ Unleashing Perception-Time Scaling to Multimodal Reasoning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. As shown, when <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is appropriately set (<em id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=3</annotation></semantics></math> as used in our experiments), the exponential reward remains low and varies smoothly for large relative errors (<em id="A3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> &gt;0.6), while exhibiting a steep increase for small relative errors (<em id="A3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> &lt;0.2). Compared to the linear reward, this design encourages the model to make high-precision estimations. We further conduct an ablation study on the choice of <math id="A3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> by comparing model performance under <math id="A3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Table <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Definition of Perception Ratio ‣ Unleashing Perception-Time Scaling to Multimodal Reasoning Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>, both smaller and larger values of <math id="A3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> lead to degraded performance compared to our chosen setting. We suggest that when <math id="A3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is too small, the model receives overly high rewards even at the beginning of training, reducing its motivation to improve. Conversely, when <math id="A3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is too large, the reward curve becomes overly flat in the high-error region, where model’s predictions commonly fall in the early stages of training, making it difficult for the model to determine a clear direction for improvement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.fig3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A3.T7" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:190.8pt;">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<table id="A3.T7.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A3.T7.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T7.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="A3.T7.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></td>
<td id="A3.T7.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="A3.T7.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DisTANCE</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T7.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T7.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">RA<sub id="A3.T7.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="A3.T7.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">0.1</span></sub></td>
<td id="A3.T7.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">RA<math id="A3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{avg}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>avg</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{avg}}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T7.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T7.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Qwen2.5-VL-7B-PTS</td>
<td id="A3.T7.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A3.T7.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">64.7</span></td>
<td id="A3.T7.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="A3.T7.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">88.3</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T7.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.T7.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">+ symbolic granularity</td>
<td id="A3.T7.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">62.7</td>
<td id="A3.T7.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">83.5</td></tr>
